/** @page dataframe_dense_columnarguide Dense Buffers And Columnar Results

    @tableofcontents

    DataFrames can be constructed from dense \c buffer<T> values for primitive
    numeric, boolean, and string columns. Buffer-backed columns keep a reference to the
    original dense storage, so \c getColumnBuffer(), \c toColumnarResult(), and
    \c fromColumnarResult() can move primitive columns between DataFrame,
    @ref buffer "buffer<T>", and
    @ref Qore::SQL::ColumnarResult "ColumnarResult" without first boxing every
    cell into a list. Fixed-scale decimal columns use \c buffer<decimal128>.
    Date, timestamp, and duration columns use \c buffer<int64> with
    ColumnarResult schema metadata when they come from dense columnar sources,
    storing microseconds and materializing Qore \c date values when read as
    rows or lists. Nested arrays, fixed-size lists, structs, maps, and
    dictionary-compatible values keep recursive ColumnarResult schema metadata
    and list/hash values; \c buffer<T> remains a primitive dense-vector type.

    @code{.py}
%requires dataframe

DataFrame::DataFrame scores({
    "id": new buffer<int64>((1001, 1002, 1003, 1004)),
    "score": new buffer<float64>((95.5, 88.0, 92.0, 81.5)),
    "active": new buffer<bool>((True, False, True, True)),
    "name": new buffer<*string>(("Alice", "Bob", "Carol", NOTHING)),
});

buffer<int64> ids = scores.getColumnBuffer("id");
buffer<*string> names = scores.getColumnBuffer("name");
ColumnarResult columns = scores.toColumnarResult();
buffer<float64> dense_scores = columns.getColumn("score");
buffer<*string> dense_names = columns.getColumn("name");

ColumnarResult timestamps = ColumnarResult::fromColumnHash({
    "created_at": (2026-01-01T00:00:00Z, 2026-01-02T00:00:00Z),
}, {
    "created_at": {"native_type": "timestamp with time zone", "nullable": False},
});
buffer<int64> created_at_us = timestamps.getColumn("created_at");
DataFrame::DataFrame timestamp_df = DataFrame::DataFrame::fromColumnarResult(timestamps);

buffer<bool> high_score = dense_scores >= 90.0;
buffer<*bool> named = dense_names == "Alice";
ColumnarResult selected_columns = columns.filter(high_score);
DataFrame::DataFrame selected = DataFrame::DataFrame::fromColumnarResult(selected_columns);
    @endcode

    ColumnarResult masks can be composed in native code with
    @ref Qore::SQL::ColumnarResult::combineMasks() "combineMasks()" and
    @ref Qore::SQL::ColumnarResult::invertMask() "invertMask()". Nullable columns can use
    @ref Qore::SQL::ColumnarResult::nullMask() "nullMask()" to build dense masks without row
    materialization. This is useful
    when code receives typed columns dynamically and cannot rely on parse-time
    vectorized operator resolution for a column variable:

    @code{.py}
buffer<bool> high = columns.getColumn("score") >= 90.0;
buffer<bool> active = columns.getColumn("active") == True;
buffer<bool> selected_mask = columns.combineMasks(high, active, "&&");
ColumnarResult high_active = columns.filter(selected_mask);
ColumnarResult missing_scores = columns.filter(columns.nullMask("score"));
ColumnarResult first_page = columns.slice(offset: 0, count: 1000);
ColumnarResult ranked_rows = columns.take((3, 0, 2));
DataFrame::DataFrame ranked_df = scores.takeRows((3, 0, 2));
    @endcode

    Use @ref Qore::SQL::ColumnarResult::take() "ColumnarResult::take()" and
    @ref Qore::DataFrame::DataFrame::takeRows() "DataFrame::takeRows()" when row order
    matters, for example after ranking model scores. These APIs gather explicit
    row indices and keep primitive columns in dense buffer form.

    Use @ref Qore::DataFrame::DataFrame::concat() "DataFrame::concat()" with
    \c axis:1 for horizontal concat. This shares existing column data when
    possible and rejects mismatched row counts or duplicate output column names:

    @code{.py}
DataFrame::DataFrame left({"id": new buffer<int64>((1, 2, 3))});
DataFrame::DataFrame right({"score": new buffer<float64>((90.0, 80.0, 70.0))});
DataFrame::DataFrame combined = DataFrame::DataFrame::concat((left, right), axis: 1);
    @endcode

    DataFrame kernels still maintain the module's existing internal vectors for
    compatibility with groupby, joins, window functions, and ML conversion.
    Therefore source buffers are preserved for columnar export and dense column
    access. Row filtering preserves primitive buffer-backed columns by creating
    filtered dense buffers, and contiguous row slicing preserves them with
    zero-copy buffer views. Operations that reorder, aggregate, or reshape rows
    may allocate new column storage.
*/
